WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.820
 Today we'll talk about the history of neural networks from 1943 to present-day 2019.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:14.640
 Although the study of the human brain is thousands of years old, the first step towards neural

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:20.400
 networks took place in 1943 when Warren McCulloch, a neurophysiologist and a young

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:27.000
 mathematician, Walter Pitts, wrote a paper on how neurons might work. They modeled a

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:34.440
 simple neural network with electrical circuits. In 1949, Donald Hebb reinforced

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:39.840
 the concept of neurons in his book, The Organization of Behavior. He pointed out

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:48.000
 that neural pathways are strengthened each time they are used. In the 1950s,

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.680
 Nathaniel Rochester from the IBM Research Laboratories alleged first effort to

00:00:53.680 --> 00:01:01.120
 simulate a neural network. In 1956, the Dartmouth Summer Research Project on

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:08.560
 Artificial Intelligence, or AI, provided a boost to both AI and neural networks.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:14.560
 This simulated research in AI and the much lower-level neural processing of the brain.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:22.000
 In 1957, John Von Neumann suggested imitating simple

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:27.280
 neuron functions by using the telegraph relays, or vacuum tubes.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:36.000
 Moving on to 1958, where Frank Rosenblatt, who was a neurobiologist for now,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:43.440
 and began work on the preceptron. He was basically intrigued with the operation of the eye of a fly.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:51.440
 Much of the processing which tells a fly to flee is done in its eye. The preceptron, which resulted from this research,

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.440
 was built in hardware and is the oldest neural network still in use today.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:02.880
 A single layer of perceptron was found to be useful in classifying a continuous

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:04.880
 valued set of inputs into one of the two classes.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.880
 The preceptron computes a weighted sum of the inputs,

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:14.880
 subtracts the threshold, and passes one of two possible values out as the result.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:23.040
 In 1959, Bernard Woodrow and Marcin Hoff of Stanford developed models they called

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:29.920
 Adeline and Madeline. These models were named for their use of the multiple adaptive linear elements.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.040
 Madeline was actually the first neural network to be applied to a real-world problem.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:42.720
 It's an adaptive filter which eliminates echoes on phone lines. This neural network is still in commercial use.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:54.000
 In 1969, Marvin Minsky and Seymour Papert proved that the preceptor to be limited in their book "Perceptrons".

00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:04.160
 Progress on neural network research actually halted due to fear, unfulfilled claims, etc. until about 1981.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:09.280
 This caused respected voices to critique the neural network research.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:15.920
 The result was a halt to much of the funding and this period of stunted growth lasted through 1981.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:24.160
 In 1982, John Hopkins presented a paper to the National Academy of Sciences.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:30.800
 His approach to create useful devices was great and he was basically likable, articulate, and charismatic.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:40.400
 In 1982, additionally, the U.S.-Japan Conference on Cooperative Competitive Neural Networks took place,

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:46.400
 at which Japan announced their fifth-generation effort, which resulted in the U.S. really worrying about

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:50.000
 being left behind and soon funding was flowing once again.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:04:00.880
 In 1985, American Institute of Physics began what has become an annual meeting of Neural Networks for Computing.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:09.120
 And by 1987, which was two years into it, the Institute of Electrical and Electronic Engineering, IEEE,

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:14.800
 its first international conference on neural networks, drew more than 1,800 attendees.

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:27.440
 In 1997, a recurrent neural network framework, long-short-term memory, LSTM, was proposed by Schimberger and Hochreicher.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:35.520
 In 1998, Jan LeCun published "Gradient-based learning applied to document recognition."

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:42.560
 Several steps have actually taken place in order to get to where we are now.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:47.840
 And today, neural networks are discussed really regularly, and the future is here.

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:52.800
 Currently, most neural network development is simply proving that the principle works.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:57.840
 However, this research is developing neural networks that, due to processing limitations,

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:02.960
 take weeks to learn. But the future is here, and it's moving really quickly.

